OD(1)                          P32W Unix                           OD(1)

NAME
       od - dump a file in hexadecimal or as characters

SYNOPSIS
       od [-c | -x] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       od writes the files, or the standard input if none is named
       (or for a file named -), to the standard output in a readable
       form, 16 bytes to a line. Several files are dumped as one
       stream, as if they had been joined by cat. Each line begins
       with the offset of its first byte, in octal (7 digits). A
       last line gives the length of the input, also as an octal
       offset.

OPTIONS
       -x     each byte as two hexadecimal digits (the default)
       -c     each byte as a character: printable ASCII as itself,
              newline, tab, return, backspace, form feed and NUL as
              \n \t \r \b \f and \0, any other byte as three
              octal digits

       Any other option is a usage error.

EXAMPLES
       od a.out | head               the start of a program
       od -c notes.txt               find stray control characters

       For example, echo hi | od -c prints

              0000000    h   i  \n
              0000003

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened
       (od says why and goes on with the next).

SEE ALSO
       cat(1), cmp(1), a.out(5)

BUGS
       Unlike V7 od, the default is hexadecimal bytes, not octal
       words. There are no -o, -d, -b or -h options, no offset
       argument, and no * for repeated lines. Only one option is
       understood.
